<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- MATH 1554 Studio Worksheet 07: Quadratic Forms (LN 2.2, 2.3)          -->
<!-- Converted from WS/WS07_22_23.tex                                      -->
<!-- Each <page> corresponds to a \newpage in the original LaTeX.          -->
<worksheet xml:id="ws-quadratic-forms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Quadratic Forms (LN 2.2, 2.3)</title>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ws-qf-poll" workspace="3in">
      <title>Poll Question</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          True or false: <m>Q(x,y) = 8x^2 + 2y^2 - 8xy \gt 0</m> for all values
          of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Hint: plot it in Desmos 3D! And: how does this exercise relate to
          symmetric matrices?</em>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Expressing Quadratic Forms in Terms of Symmetric Matrices</title>

      <definition xml:id="ws-qf-def-quadratic-form">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A <term>quadratic form</term> is a function <m>Q : \R^n \to \R</m>,
            given by
            <md>
              Q(\vec x) = \vec x\,^T A \vec x =
              \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \amp x_2 \amp \cdots \amp x_n \end{pmatrix}
              \begin{pmatrix}
                a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
                a_{12} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
                \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
                a_{1n} \amp a_{2n} \amp \cdots \amp a_{nn}
              \end{pmatrix}
              \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{pmatrix}
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            Matrix <m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m> and symmetric, and
            <m>\vec x</m> is a variable vector in <m>\R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Example: express
        <m>Q = 3x_1^2 + x_2^2 + 8x_3^2 + 4x_1x_2 - 6x_2x_3</m>
        in the form <m>Q = \vec x\,^T A \vec x</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>The Principle Axes Theorem</title>

      <theorem xml:id="ws-qf-thm-principal-axes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix then there exists an orthogonal
            change of variable <m>\vec x = P \vec y</m> that transforms
            <m>\vec x\,^T A \vec x</m> to <m>\vec y\,^T D \vec y</m> with no
            cross-product terms.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        <em>Proof in the lecture videos/textbook.</em>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <!-- NOTE: this exercise is deliberately a direct child of <page>, NOT     -->
    <!-- nested inside the <paragraphs> above.  Nesting an <exercise> inside   -->
    <!-- <paragraphs> makes PreTeXt 2.45.0 treat it as an *inline* exercise    -->
    <!-- and emit \begin{inlineexercise} with 4 arguments to an environment    -->
    <!-- defined with 3, which prints the @xml:id as literal body text.        -->
    <!-- As a direct child of <page> it is correctly a worksheet exercise.     -->
    <exercise xml:id="ws-qf-change-of-variable">
      <title>Example</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider: <m>Q = x^2 + y^2 + 2kxy</m> where <m>k \in \R</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Make a change of variable that transforms the quadratic form into
            another quadratic form that has no cross-product terms.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For what values of <m>k</m> will <m>Q</m> satisfy
            <m>Q \ge 0</m> for any <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <conclusion>
        <p>
          <em>Tip: it may help to plot the surfaces in Desmos 3D to see what
          the surface looks like as you change the value of <m>k</m>.</em>
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Classifying Quadratic Surfaces</title>

      <p>
        Our goal: <alert>solve optimization problems with quadratic
        functions.</alert>
      </p>

      <p>
        Take for example the surface:
        <md>
          Q = x^2 + y^2 = \vec x\,^T \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix} \vec x
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        If we set <m>Q</m> to be constant, <m>Q = k^2</m>, we obtain the
        equation of a circle whose radius is <m>k^2</m>.
        <md>
          k^2 = x^2 + y^2
        </md>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="ws-qf-fig-paraboloid">
        <caption>The surface <m>Q = x^2 + y^2</m>.</caption>
        <image xml:id="ws-qf-img-paraboloid" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A circular paraboloid opening upward with its vertex at the origin.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional plot of the surface <m>Q = x^2 + y^2</m>,
              drawn over a circular disc. The surface is a bowl-shaped circular
              paraboloid opening upward with a unique minimum at the origin,
              shaded from dark at the vertex through orange to light at the
              outer rim.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
              \begin{axis}[
                axis line style = very thick, smooth,
                xmin=-4, ymin=-4, zmin=0, xmax=4, ymax=4, zmax=10,
                axis lines=center,
                xticklabels={,,}, yticklabels={,,}, zticklabels={,,},
                xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty, ztick=\empty,
                colormap/hot,
              ]
                \addplot3[
                  surf,
                  shader=flat,
                  draw=none,
                  restrict z to domain=0:9,
                  data cs=polar,
                  domain=0:360,
                  y domain=0:3,
                  samples=40,
                ] (x, y, y^2);
              \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The surface above has a unique minimum at the origin. What about other
        surfaces?
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="ws-qf-def-definiteness">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A quadratic form <m>Q</m> is
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>positive definite</term> if <m>Q \gt 0</m> for all
                  <m>\vec x \ne \vec 0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>negative definite</term> if <m>Q \lt 0</m> for all
                  <m>\vec x \ne \vec 0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>positive semidefinite</term> if <m>Q \ge 0</m> for all
                  <m>\vec x</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>negative semidefinite</term> if <m>Q \le 0</m> for all
                  <m>\vec x</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>indefinite</term> if <m>Q</m> takes on positive and
                  negative values for <m>\vec x \ne \vec 0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <theorem xml:id="ws-qf-thm-eigenvalue-classification">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix with eigenvalues
            <m>\lambda_i</m>, then <m>Q = \vec x\,^T A \vec x</m> is
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>positive definite</term> when all eigenvalues are
                  positive
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>negative definite</term> when all eigenvalues are
                  negative
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <term>indefinite</term> when at least one eigenvalue is
                  negative and at least one eigenvalue is positive
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ws-qf-classify" workspace="4in">
      <title>Example</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume <m>\vec x \in \R^3</m>. Construct the matrix of the quadratic
          form, and classify the quadratic form.
          <md>
            Q = 4x_3^2 - 2x_1x_2
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

</worksheet>